problems.problem:
Two players, Alan and Bea, play as follows. Alan chooses two positive integers $a$ and $b$. Then, knowing $a$ and $b$, Bea colours all the positive integers with two colours. If, after this, it is possible to choose (at least) two positive integers $x$, $y$ of the same colour such that $y-x$ equals $a$ or equals $b$, Alan wins. Otherwise, Bea wins. Determine who wins for each of the following choices of $a$ and $b$:

a) $7$ and $11$;

b) $13$ and $20$;

c) $8$ and $12$;

d) $24$ and $40$.
